 course put  enormous pressure on the electricity  sector in India we've seen in the United  States in Australia in France in Germany  nuclear power plants and other power  plants have to shut down an interrupt  energy supply because of an absence of  available water resources ok so it's a  real problem and it's very much alive  once you throw land into the mix and you  bring in the consequences for  agricultural production and food prices  all bets are off and in 2009 we saw what  happens when you have a spike in staple  food products and so the oh I should  also point out that that there is no  particular technology that is immune  from this problem right the most obvious  is hydroelectricity alright but that's  that it doesn't stop there any kind of  thermal power generation is vulnerable  to scarce water resources anything that  uses water as a coolant is vulnerable to  ambient increase in ambient water  temperature okay fracking is another one  biofuels you know it's off the chart in  terms of water resource consumption so I  think understanding the Nexus is  starting to act on it is going to have  three consequences the first is we're  already seeing this is we're going to  see changes in standards around energy  efficiency around where you can cite  electricity generation plants and  there's going to be a whole suite of  water triggers within the regulatory  arrangements to ensure that sensible  decisions are made we're also going to  see things like code generation where  instead of using fresh water resources  we might see a desalination used and  we'll just cop the the energy cost of  that we may well see more efficient and  interconnected electricity markets to  promote greater  we have a national electricity market in  Australia so when we can't use our hydro  facility down south we can buy my energy  from up north right now that's great for  us but it's my contention that we might  actually see greater more flexible  competitive integrated energy markets  and electricity markets in places like  South East Asia okay where this issue  starts to get more and more live as they  develop further the most exciting aspect  of this to me and I share Dan's  enthusiasm about renewable energy  technology is that it just makes that  argument to use renewable energy  technologies that much more compelling  ok now the knock-on consequences of that  which I get very tickled pink about is  the potential for distributed energy  storage technologies to be a support  mechanism to enable that renewable  energy penetration and I don't want to  trample all over this afternoon's panel  but suffice it to say that in Australia  in the United States in parts of Europe  there have been some amazing initiatives  just in the last three years around  pilot projects for distributed energy  storage and what that is precipitating  in Australia at any rate is new business  models so just this week in Australia  the macquarie bank group applied to be  an energy retailer right it applied for  electricity retail license all right so  it's my contention that if we see more  renewables we see more distributed  energy storage we're going to see  different actors different people will  be producing energy and that's going to  require a whole shift in our regulatory  framework so that was the second issue I  forgot to start with that the third  issue is the persistent price  differentials that exist between regions  for electricity okay in Europe and in  Japan they pay to three at the moment  three times as much for their  electricity the world energy outlook  report from last year suggests that by  2035 they'll still be paying twice as  much  for electricity as the United States has  profound implications for energy  intensive production particularly  energy-intensive trade exposed  commodities and so where I think this  brings us to if we take the water energy  and more recently the land Nexus  combined with the penetration of  renewable energy technologies and what I  think that means for energy storage plus  the potential for greater incentive for  integrated grits more competitive more  flexible integrated electricity grids in  places where they're not currently  integrated I think we see a number of  consequences and and like Neil please  don't make investment decisions based on  this I think we're going to see  fundamental reform and in virtually  transformation of the electricity sector  in a number of ways we're going to see  reform of pricing particularly to  account for the externalities of water  impacts we're going to see new business  models new actors but new risks that  come with those new business models and  I'll go into that in detail in question  time if you like I think we're going to  see two weird things happen you know and  as a political scientist of course I'm  you know I'm focused on the role of the  state and who's doing what I think we're  going to see two things happen one is  we're going to have increasingly  decentralised distributed electricity  sectors so we've got more actors we've  got really innovative different business  models and we've got distributed energy  technologies renewable plus storage but  I think we're at the international level  we're going to see much more integration  and cooperation and collaboration to  allow for those very real climate risks  and water risks simply to bring about  greater energy security so I think it's  an exciting space and I'm having to  explore any or all of that in questions  you
 

